---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const claims = [
  ["They were living in the Cape Colony", "doc", "Confirmed", "Every birth, marriage and death in the line from 1788 to 1907 is in the Cape Colony — Graaff-Reinet, Cradock, Barkly East, Dordrecht, Indwe."],
  ["They were rebels fighting for the Boer republics", "inf", "The household is documented; the family is not", "The Cape Rebel register has now been searched. The man in whose house they married in July 1901 — D. W. Schoeman of Caerlaverock — was tried as a Cape Rebel the month before. But NO Booysen of this district appears on the roll; the 26 Booysens on it are all Northern Cape."],
  ["Some Booysens were joiners on the British side", "lore", "Searched; inconclusive", "The national register of 952 joiners contains exactly one Booysen — J. J. Booysen of HEILBRON, Orange Free State. A real man, but not a Cape colonist and not of this district."],
  ["Our great-grandfather changed the spelling", "doc", "The change is recorded", "MyHeritage carries “Former name: Booysen” on Willem Hermanus Booyzen (1851–1905) as a formal fact. The motive is testimony; the surname distribution independently fits it."],
  ["Our great-great-grandfather was shot and killed by the British", "lore", "Disproved for both candidates", "Petrus Jacobus died in 1884, fifteen years before the war. Willem Hermanus died in 1905 of a stomach ulcer after fourteen months\u2019 illness — the death registration is now read. Whoever the story means, it is neither of them."],
  ["The women and children were put in a concentration camp", "lore", "Untested", "Camp registers survive and are indexed by name. This is the single most answerable claim on the list and it has not been searched."],
  ["The farm was burned under Kitchener\u2019s scorched earth", "inf", "One new piece of evidence", "No farm has been identified. But he is a Boer — a farmer — at his marriage in 1879, and a Mason at his death in 1905. A man who works land and then works stone has often lost the land, and the war sits in that interval."],
  ["Great-great-grandmother put the family on a train to Johannesburg; the boys went onto the mines", "inf", "Consistent with the record", "Birthplaces move from Barkly East (1907) to Johannesburg (1932). The family is at Forest Hill and Mayfair by the 1940s. No mine record found."],
];
---
<Base title="The rebels"
      eyebrow="Barkly East and Dordrecht, 1899–1902"
      dek="One paragraph of family testimony carries this archive. It names a war, a betrayal, an execution, a camp, a burned farm and a train. Here it is in full, and here is each of its claims taken apart and tested.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The testimony, in full</h2>
  <blockquote style="font-size:1.02em">
    "According to my dad the story goes that they were living in the Cape Colony and were rebels fighting
    on the side of the Boer republics at the turn of the century but there were some Booysens who were
    joiners, fighting on the side of the British, so our great grandfather changed the spelling to Booyzen
    so as not to be associated with them. Our great great grandfather was shot and killed by the British
    and all the women and children were put in a concentration camp and the family farm in the Eastern
    Cape was burned to the ground with Kitchener's scorched earth policy. After the war our great great
    grandmother (with nothing but a pumpkin to eat) put herself and her kids on a train to Johannesburg
    where the boys initially all got jobs on the mines to make ends meet."
  </blockquote>
  <p class="cap">Recorded on the MyHeritage profile of Willem Hermanus "William Henry" Booyzen and
    attributed there to <strong>Tersia Booyzen</strong>, who gives her own father as her source. It is
    therefore third-hand at best, and none the worse for saying so.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Eight claims, one at a time</h2>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:19rem">The claim</th><th>Status</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{claims.map(([c, k, s, w]) => (
        <tr><td><strong>{c}</strong></td>
            <td><span class={"chip " + k}>{s}</span></td>
            <td>{w}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What the documents did to the story</h2>
  <p class="dek">Four civil records were read for the first time in September 2026. They cost the testimony
    one of its central claims, and handed it a new piece of support it never had.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Lost: the murdered forebear</h3>
      <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <b>Willem Hermanus Booysen died of an ulcer of the
        stomach</b> at Doorn Street, Indwe, on 13 September 1905, after <b>fourteen months</b> of illness,
        attended by <b>Dr W. C. Macdonald</b>, with his eldest son present at the death.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Neither he nor his father was shot. The claim survives only if it belongs
        to a man this archive has not yet identified.
        <a href={u("/disputed/")}>The three surviving readings &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Gained: a farmer who became a mason</h3>
      <p>At his marriage in <b>1879</b> his occupation is <b>Boer</b> — farmer, of Lady Grey. At his death
        in <b>1905</b> it is <b>Mason</b>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> A man who works land and then works
        stone has, in the interval, very often <b>lost the land</b>. The war sits in that interval. It is
        the first documentary support the burned-farm claim has ever had — and it is circumstantial.
        <a href={u("/documents/")}>The documents &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>And a fourth thing, from 1940.</b> Kathleen Barry's death certificate records her as a
    <b>"Military Pensioner"</b>. Her husband died in 1905 of an ulcer, so it is not his service — most
    likely a son's, in the First World War. A family that remembers fighting the British ends with its
    matriarch drawing a military pension in a British dominion.
    <a href={u("/documents/")}>The certificate &rarr;</a>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:16px">
    And a fifth, which belongs to the name rather than the war: on the 1905 form the clerk writes the
    dead man as <b>Booysen</b>, and four lines below his son signs as <b>Booyzen</b>.
    <b>Both spellings are on one sheet of paper, three years after the war ended.</b>
    <a href={u("/name/")}>The name &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="wagon" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>They lived in the most rebellious corner of the Colony</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This is the strongest thing the archive can currently say, and it does not depend on the family at
      all. It depends on where they were.</p>
    <p>A <b>Cape Rebel</b> was a British subject of the Cape Colony who took up arms with the Transvaal and
      Orange Free State against British forces — that is, a man committing <b>high treason</b> in his own
      country. Roughly <b>15,433</b> of them have been documented.</p>
    <p>The districts that produced them are not evenly spread. <b>Burgersdorp alone produced 1,048.</b>
      <b>Aliwal North and Lady Grey together produced 1,268.</b> And
      <b>Barkly East and Dordrecht supplied about 550 combatants before the Battle of Stormberg</b> in
      December 1899 — with a commando known as the <b>Barkly East Rebels</b> under Commandant J. F. de Wet.</p>
    <p>Now place the family on that map:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:20px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>District</th><th>Rebel contribution</th><th>The family there</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Burgersdorp</strong></td><td class="m">1,048 rebels</td>
          <td>six Barry children born 1847–1863; George Augustus Kolbe buried in the Old Cemetery, 1844</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Barkly East</strong></td><td class="m">part of ~550, own commando</td>
          <td>Kathleen Barry b. 1859; four Booyzen children b. 1885–1907; Margarietha Barry b. 1880; Anna Pretorius b. 1905; John Augustus Barry died at Manor Owen, 1903</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Dordrecht</strong></td><td class="m">part of ~550; in rebel hands 1899</td>
          <td>Kathleen Barry christened there, 1859; Hendrik Nicholaas Barry b. 1857; James Henry Barry married there, 1867</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lady Grey</td><td class="m">part of 1,268</td><td>Richard Peter Barry b. 1866</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <blockquote>
    The family was not near the Cape rebellion. <strong>The family was inside it</strong> — living, marrying
    and burying across the four districts that supplied the largest share of its manpower.
  </blockquote>
  <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> This establishes overwhelming <em>opportunity and milieu</em>.
    It does not put a rifle in any named ancestor's hands, and the archive will not pretend otherwise until
    a name is found on a roll. <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>What would settle it &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The rolls have now been searched — and one name came back</h2>
  <p class="dek">The <strong>Anglo-Boer War Museum</strong> at Bloemfontein publishes fifteen registers,
    among them <strong>Cape Rebels — 12,792 records</strong>, <strong>Joiners — 952</strong> and a
    <strong>Farm List — 10,282</strong>. Searched 9 September 2026.</p>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">No Booysen of this district is on the roll</h3>
      <p><b>26 Booysens</b> appear among the Cape Rebels. Their addresses are <b>Kenhardt, Campbell,
        Koopmansfontein, Daniel's Kuil, Vaalgras, Blauwputs, Aberdeen</b> — the <b>Northern Cape and
        Griqualand West</b>, two to five hundred miles west of Barkly East.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><b>Not one is from Barkly East, Dordrecht, Wodehouse, Indwe, Lady Grey
        or Elliot.</b> <span class="chip lore">Negative result</span></p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">One tease: <b>David Schalk Booysen of Kapteinskop</b> — a
        forename pair that runs in this family. But the district is wrong, and the surname is common.
        It proves nothing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And one Booysen joiner, from the wrong colony</h3>
      <p>The testimony says <em>"there were some Booysens who were joiners."</em> The national register of
        <b>952 joiners</b> contains <b>exactly one Booysen</b> — <b>J. J. Booysen of Heilbron</b>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Heilbron is in the <b>Orange Free State</b>. A real man, but a Free State
        burgher, not a Cape colonist and not from this district.
        <b>The claim is neither confirmed nor refuted.</b></p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px"><b>"Booyzen" with a z returns zero records in all fifteen
        registers</b> — but read that cautiously. The z-form is 83 times rarer; its absence from a
        12,792-name index is what base rates predict, and proves nothing on its own.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:36px">Then the farm name was searched instead of the surname</h3>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:16rem">Cape Rebel register</th><th>Occupation</th><th>Trial date</th><th>Address</th><th>District</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>SCHOEMAN, DANIEL WILLEM</strong></td><td>Plaasboer</td>
          <td class="m"><strong>Junie 1901</strong></td><td><strong>CAERLAVEROCK</strong></td>
          <td>Wodehouse – Barkley-Oos</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>KLOPPER, CHRISTIAAN F.</strong></td><td>Plaasboer</td>
          <td class="m"><strong>Junie 1901</strong></td><td><strong>CAERLAVEROCK</strong></td>
          <td>Wodehouse – Barkley-Oos</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:22px">Now set that beside the marriage register this archive had already read:</p>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:14px">
    <strong>8 July 1901</strong> — Petrus Jacobus Pretorius married Magrita Wilhelmina Elizabeth Barry
    <em>"in <strong>woonhuis D. Schoeman</strong> te <strong>Caerlaverock</strong>"</em>, district Barkly
    Oost — witnessed and signed by <strong>D. W. SCHOEMAN</strong>.
  </blockquote>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:26px">
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <b>Daniel Willem Schoeman, farmer of Caerlaverock, was tried
    as a Cape Rebel in June 1901. The family married in his house the following month, and he signed the
    register as a witness.</b><br /><br />
    Until now this archive could say only that the family <em>lived in a rebel district</em>. It can now
    name <b>the man, the farm, the district and the month of his treason trial</b> — and show the family
    in his house, weeks later. <b>A second rebel, Christiaan F. Klopper, farmed the same place and was
    tried the same month. Caerlaverock was not incidentally connected to the rebellion; it was a rebel
    farm.</b>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">The Farm List was searched too — and the answer is "wrong register"</h3>
  <p class="dek">The museum's <strong>Farm List (10,282 records)</strong> looked like the obvious place to
    test the burned-farm claim. It is not.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:14rem">Searched</th><th>Farm List</th><th>Elsewhere</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Caerlaverock</strong></td><td class="m">0</td><td>Cape Rebels <strong>2</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Manorowen</strong></td><td class="m">0</td><td><strong>0 in all fifteen registers</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wolwekloof</td><td class="m">0</td><td>Cape Rebels (Schoeman)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p>At first sight that reads as a negative result. <b>It is not a result at all.</b></p>
    <p>The one Booysen record in that register resolves to: <b>Booysen, Petrus Gerhardus · Farm
      Blydschap · Number 316 · District Kroonstad · Morgen 2002 · SRds 56 · Number of Owners.</b>
      Farm number, morgen, square roods, owners — <b>a land and property roll</b>, not a record of
      destruction.</p>
    <p>And it does not cover the Cape. Sampling <b>about 1,280 records</b> from across the full 10,282,
      every district returned is in the <b>Orange Free State</b>: <b>Winburg, Fauresmith, Vrede,
      Rouxville, Bethlehem, Bloemfontein, Hoopstad, Kroonstad, Philippolis</b> — and nothing else. A
      control settles it: <b>"Dordrecht" returns 670 Cape Rebels and 0 Farm List records.</b></p>
    <blockquote>
      The Farm List is an <strong>Orange Free State land register</strong>. Caerlaverock and Manorowen are
      <strong>Cape</strong> farms. Their absence from it carries <strong>no information whatever</strong>
      — it is a category error, not a finding, and this archive will not present it as one.
    </blockquote>
    <p><b>So the burned-farm claim remains completely untested</b>, and this database was never capable of
      testing it. The right sources are <b>Cape Colony compensation claims</b>, the post-war
      <b>farm-burning returns</b>, and the Cape's own district and refugee-camp records. None has been
      touched. <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">What the Schoeman finding does not establish</h3>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p><b>1. It still does not put a Booysen or a Barry on a rebel roll.</b> The rebel here is the
      <em>host</em>, not the family. The testimony's claim that <em>"they were rebels"</em> remains
      uncorroborated <b>for the family itself</b>, and that distinction matters.</p>
    <p><b>2. It does not prove he was "Commandant D. Schoeman"</b> of Labuschagne's Nek. The record's
      <em>Commando</em> field reads <b>N/A</b>, and there is a <b>cluster</b> of D. W. Schoemans tried in
      the same division — <b>Daniel Wilhelmus of Wolwekloof</b> and <b>Daniel W. of Waschbank</b>, both
      farmers, both tried <b>1 May 1901</b>, plus a <b>Danie J. W.</b> tried in July 1902. They may be
      relatives, or one man entered several times. <b>Unresolved.</b></p>
    <p><b>3.</b> <span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> A June 1901 trial with <b>no sentence recorded</b>
      fits <b>Class 2</b> treatment — rank and file, disenfranchised for five years rather than
      imprisoned. That would explain how he was at home hosting a wedding in July. Not documented.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The joiners register holds exactly one Booysen, and he is nowhere near here</h2>
  <p class="dek">The testimony's whole motive rests on there having been Booysen joiners. There is a
    register of them.</p>
  <p>
    The Anglo-Boer War Museum keeps a <b>Joiners</b> register of <b>952 names</b>. Searched for
    <b>Booysen</b> it returns <b>one</b>:
  </p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin:18px 0">
    <table style="width:100%">
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:12rem">Surname</td><td><strong>BOOYSEN</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Name</td><td><strong>J.J.</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Farm / Town</td><td><strong>Heilbron</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Source</td><td>Vrystaatse Argieflys (Free State archive list)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <b>Heilbron is in the northern Orange Free State</b> — roughly four hundred miles from Barkly East,
    in a different country at the time. <b>Booyzen returns nothing at all.</b>
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>What this does to the motive.</strong> The testimony says the spelling was
      changed because <em>"there were some Booysens who were joiners"</em>. The register of joiners holds
      <b>one</b> Booysen in the whole of South Africa, and he is a Free State man.</p>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0">That is not fatal to the story. Joiner lists are notoriously incomplete,
      and a family need only have <em>believed</em> the association existed for it to have acted on it.
      But the archive can no longer say the joiners are unexamined. <b>"Some Booysens" is not what the
      record shows.</b></p>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="thorn" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>The Barrys were searched — and the family <em>is</em> on the Cape Rebel roll</h2>
  <p class="dek">The archive had searched Booysen and Booyzen and come back with nothing from this
    district. It had written down the obvious next step and not taken it: <em>"the surname BARRY, which
    has not been searched at all."</em></p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin:20px 0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:14rem">Cape Rebel</th><th>Occupation</th><th>Address</th><th>District</th><th class="m">Tried</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>BARRY, FRED J.</strong></td><td>plaasboer</td><td><strong>Ivanhoe</strong></td>
            <td><strong>Barkley-Oos, Wodehouse</strong></td><td class="m"><strong>June 1901</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>BARRY, RICHARD PETER</strong></td><td>plaasboer</td><td><strong>Jerpoint</strong></td>
            <td class="m">—</td><td class="m">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Barry, Adolf</td><td>plaasboer</td><td>Keimoes</td><td class="m">— (Northern Cape)</td><td class="m">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Barry, Thomas Andrew</td><td class="m">—</td><td>Vlakteplaats</td><td>Oudtshoorn</td><td class="m">21 Mar 1902</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>Two of the four are in this family's own district, and one of them bears a
      name this archive has already read on a document.</strong></p>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0"><b>Richard Peter Barry</b> is <b>Kathleen Barry's brother</b> — the
      <b>"R. P. Barry"</b> who signed his mother's death notice in 1915 as <em>"Son in whose house
      deceased died"</em>, at <b>Manorowen, Barkly East</b>. A Richard Peter Barry, <em>plaasboer</em>,
      is on the roll of Cape Rebels.</p>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0"><b>Fred J. Barry</b> farmed <b>Ivanhoe</b> in <b>Barkly East / Wodehouse</b>
      and was <b>tried in June 1901</b> — the same district, and <b>the same month</b> as
      <b>Daniel Willem Schoeman of Caerlaverock</b>, in whose house this family married five weeks later.</p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:20px">
    <span class="chip inf">Identity not proven</span> The register gives no birth dates and no parents, so
    the archive cannot yet show that these are the same men. <b>Richard Peter Barry</b> is a distinctive
    name in the right region; <b>Fred J.</b> does not match this family's <b>Frederick Fortunatus Barry</b>
    on the middle initial, and may be a cousin or no relation.
  </p>
  <blockquote>But the shape of the testimony has changed. It was never wrong that this family was
    rebel country. It was looking at the wrong surname.</blockquote>
  <p>
    The claim <em>"the family were Cape Rebels"</em> has been tested three times now. Against
    <b>Booysen</b>: twenty-six on the roll, every one of them Northern Cape. Against <b>Booyzen</b>:
    nothing, in any of the fifteen registers. Against <b>Barry</b>: <b>two men in Barkly East and
    Wodehouse, one of them tried in the month of the wedding.</b>
    <a href={u("/barry/")}>The Barrys &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Jerpoint, Ivanhoe, Manorowen, Caerlaverock</h3>
  <p>
    Four farms in one small district, named for <b>an abbey in County Kilkenny</b>, <b>a novel of Saxons
    and Normans</b>, <b>a village in Pembrokeshire</b> and <b>a castle in Dumfriesshire</b>. The archive
    had already noticed Manorowen and Caerlaverock. <b>Jerpoint is new, and it is Irish</b> — which is
    where the Barrys are supposed to have come from, and where this archive has never been able to place
    them. <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>A coincidence worth chasing: Commandant D. Schoeman</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>On <b>8 July 1901</b> — with martial law in force and the war still running —
      <b>Margarietha Wilhelmina Elizabeth Barry married Petrus Jacobus Pretorius</b>. The tree records the
      venue with unusual precision:</p>
    <blockquote>"At the home of <strong>D Schoeman</strong>, <strong>'Caerlaverock'</strong>, Barkly East
      district, Cape Colony."</blockquote>
    <p>In <b>March 1900</b>, about <b>400 Cape Rebels under Commandant D. Schoeman</b> held
      <b>Labuschagne's Nek, north of Dordrecht</b> — the next district over.</p>
    <p>The bride's mother was <b>Anna Johanna Maria Schoemann</b>. The wedding was held at a Schoeman house.
      The couple's granddaughter's line is the one that carries the rebel story.</p>
    <p>And there is now a third Schoeman, twenty-two years earlier. The <b>1879 marriage register</b> for
      Willem Hermanus Booysen and Kathleen Barry names its two witnesses:
      <b>J. Sutherland and P. Schoeman</b>.</p>
    <p><b>Then the 1901 register itself was read</b>, and it gives the man a signature:
      <b>D. W. SCHOEMAN</b>. Not "D. Schoeman" from a note, but full initials in his own hand, witnessing
      in his own house at Caerlaverock, sixteen months after the Nek.</p>
    <blockquote>
      <b>Commandant D. Schoeman</b>, Labuschagne's Nek, March 1900. &nbsp;·&nbsp;
      <b>D. W. Schoeman</b>, of Caerlaverock, Barkly East, July 1901.
    </blockquote>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Hypothesis, sharpened</span> That is a <b>specific and searchable
      identity</b> to set against the commandant lists, where before there was only an initial.
      <b>It is still not proof.</b> <a href={u("/documents/")}>The register &rarr;</a></p>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Hypothesis</span> <b>The family married in the house of a Cape Rebel
      commandant, sixteen months after he commanded on the Nek, and he was a relative by marriage.</b></p>
    <p><b>It may be nothing.</b> Schoeman is a common Afrikaner surname and "D." is one initial. But the
      district is right, the years are right, and the family connection is documented. This is the most
      promising single lead in the archive, and it is cheap to test: the rebel rolls name their
      commandants. <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What happened to rebels who were caught</h2>
  <p class="dek">Because the testimony says one of them was shot, it matters what the British actually did.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The system</h3>
      <p>Lord Roberts split surrendered rebels into <b>Class 1 — leaders</b>, who faced fines and prison,
        and <b>Class 2 — rank and file</b>, who got <b>five years' disenfranchisement</b>. The
        <b>Special Tribunals Act</b> of October 1900 handled the cases. From <b>April 1901</b> martial law
        took over and <b>military courts tried some 800 cases</b>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The sentences</h3>
      <p><b>Forty-four Cape colonists were hanged or shot for high treason.</b> <b>409</b> younger rebels
        were transported for life to <b>St Helena and Bermuda</b>. Named men from these districts were
        imprisoned — <b>Johannes de Wet</b>, three years and a £1,000 fine; <b>Octavius Vermooten</b>, a
        Dordrecht lawyer, four years in Tokai Gaol.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>Forty-four is a small, closed, checkable list.</b> If a Booysen was "shot and killed by the British"
    as a rebel, he is on it — or the story means something else: killed in action, killed in a farm
    clearance, or died in a camp. Those are different searches, and the archive should run all of them
    before it decides the testimony is wrong. <a href={u("/disputed/")}>The chronology problem &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="koppie" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>The camps</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The MyHeritage biography for Willem Hermanus Booyzen carries a long passage, quoted from a published
      history of the war, on <b>Kitchener's reasoning</b> for clearing the farms: that Boer women sustained
      the commandos with food and intelligence, that removing them would starve the war of both, and that
      men might surrender for their families' sake. <span class="cap">The passage is a copyrighted
      extract and is summarised rather than reproduced here; it is not evidence about this family, but it
      is evidence about what the family understood its own story to mean.</span></p>
    <p>Whoever attached that passage to Willem Hermanus's profile did so because they believed his wife and
      children were among the women and children it describes. <b>That belief has never been checked.</b></p>
    <p>Kathleen Barry was <b>forty</b> when the war began, with children aged from an infant to fifteen. If
      the family was cleared off a farm in the Barkly East district, she and they would appear in a camp
      register by name, with ages. <b>Those registers are indexed.</b></p>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
